### 1. Data Collection and Format

####1.1 Raw data [sub-xx]

EEG data were recorded using a 32-channel EEG cap following the 10–10 international system.
Sampling rate: 1000 Hz.
Each trial consisted of a visual cue followed by an imagery period (imagine animals, figures, or objects).
Event markers (.tsv files) include:
| onset | duration | type |…
| ----- | -------- | ---------- |…
| 12.5  | 4.0        | circle     |…
| 25.0  | 4.0        | pentagram     |…
| 37.5  | 4.0        | square     |…
| ...   | ...      | ...        |

#### 1.2 preprocessing method [code/]

EEG signals were preprocessed with MNE-Python. After applying a 1–30 Hz band-pass filter and downsampling to 500 Hz, artifacts were removed using ICA (Picard algorithm) and ICLabel classification. Non-brain components were excluded. Data were z-scored for standardization. For event-related analysis, 4-second epochs were extracted and resampled to 250 Hz. Final preprocessed epochs and labels were stored in .mat and .tsv formats ready for subsequent machine learning and statistical analysis.

#### 1.3 Experimental Stimuli [stimuli/]
Contains the visual materials used in the experiment:
animal_*, figure_*, object_*.


### 2. How to Use This Dataset
The raw EEG data were collected using the NeuSenW32 and converted into Biosemi version(.bdf) using Matlab (R2021a).The process scripts and codes used in this dataset were running in Visual Studio Code [version: 1.100.2, python version: 3.9.13].


### 3. Citation

If you use this dataset, please cite:
Gao, J., Liu, Y., Wang, F., Zhao, L., Li, T., Li, Z., Huang, K., Fu, Y. [n. d. ]. An EEG Dataset for Visual Imagery-Based Brain–Computer Interface.


